<section xml:id="sec_mult-and-division-by-t">
<title>Multiplication and Division by <m>t</m></title>
	<p>
		Sometimes the solutions to nonhomogeneous linear differential equations with constant coefficients involve the functions
		<m>t\cos bt</m>, <m>t\sin bt</m>, or <m>t^ne^{at}</m> as part of the solution. We now show how the Laplace transforms of
		<m>tf(t)</m> and <m>\frac{f(t)}{t}</m> are related to the Laplace transform of <m>f(t)</m>. The transform of <m>tf(t)</m>
		will be obtained via differentiation and the transform of <m>\frac{f(t)}{t}</m> will be obtained via integration. To be
		precise, we state the following theorems.
	</p>
	<theorem>
	<title>Multiplication by <m>\boldsymbol t</m></title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				If <m>F(s)</m> is the Laplace transform of <m>f(t)</m>, then
				<md>
					\mathcal{L}\big(tf(t)\big) = -F\,'(s)
				</md>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</theorem>
	<proof>
		<p>
			By definition we have <m>F(s) = \int_0^{\infty}f(t)e^{-st}\,dt</m>.  Leibniz's rule for partial differentiation under the
			integral sign permits us to write
			<md>
				<mrow>F\,'(s) \amp = \frac{\partial }{\partial s}\int_0^{\infty}f(t)e^{-st}\,dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = \int_0^{\infty}\frac{\partial }{\partial s}[f(t)e^{-st}]\,dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = \int_0^{\infty}[-tf(t) e^{-st}]\,dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = -\int_0^{\infty}tf(t) e^{-st}dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = -\mathcal{L}\big(tf(t)\big) </mrow>
			</md>.
		</p>
	</proof>
	<theorem xml:id="thm_div-by-t">
	<title>Division by <m>\boldsymbol t</m></title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let both <m>f(t)</m> and <m>\frac{f(t)}{t}</m> have Laplace transforms and let <m>F(s)</m> denote the transform of
				<m>f(t)</m>. If <m>\lim\limits_{t \to 0^+}\frac{f(t)}{t}</m> exists,
				<md>
					\mathcal{L}\left(\frac{f(t)}{t}\right) = \int_s^{\infty}F(\sigma)\,d\sigma
				</md>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</theorem>
	<proof>
		<p>
			Since <m>F(\sigma) = \int_0^{\infty}f(t) e^{-\sigma t}\,dt</m>, we integrate <m>F(\sigma)</m> from <m>s</m> to
			<m>\infty</m>:
			<md>
				\int_s^{\infty}F(\sigma ) d\sigma =\int_0^{\infty}\left[\int_0^{\infty}f(t) e^{-\sigma t}\,dt\right]d\sigma
			</md>.
			The order of integration in equation in the double integral is reversed:
			<md>
				<mrow>
					\int_s^{\infty}F(\sigma )\,d\sigma \amp = \int_0^{\infty}\left[\int_s^{\infty}f(t) e^{-\sigma t}d\sigma\right]dt
				</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = \int_0^{\infty}\left[\frac{-f(t)}{t}e^{-\sigma t}\Big|_{\sigma =s}^{\sigma =\infty}\right]dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = \int_0^{\infty}\frac{f(t)}{t}e^{-st}\,dt</mrow>
				<mrow>\amp = \mathcal{L}\left(\frac{f(t)}{t}\right)</mrow>
			</md>.
		</p>
	</proof>
	<example>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Show that <m>\mathcal{L}(t\cos bt) =\frac{s^2-b^2}{(s^2+b^2)^2}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(t) =\cos bt</m>, then <m>F(s) =\mathcal{L}(\cos bt) = \frac{s}{s^2+b^2}</m>. Hence, we can differentiate
				<m>F(s)</m> to obtain the desired result:
				<md>
					\mathcal{L}(t\cos bt) =-F\,'(s) =-\frac{s^2+b^2-2s^2}{(s^2+b^2)^2}=\frac{s^2-b^2}{(s^2+b^2)}
				</md>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</example>
	<example>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Show that <m>\mathcal{L}(\frac{\sin t}{t}) =\arctan \frac{1}{s}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(t) = \sin t</m> and <m>F(s) =\frac{1}{s^2+1}</m>. Since <m>\lim\limits_{t \to 0^+}\frac{\sin t}{t}=1</m>, we
				can integrate <m>F(s)</m> to obtain the desired result:
				<md>
					\mathcal{L}\left(\frac{\sin t}{t}\right) = \int_s^{\infty}\frac{d\sigma}{\sigma^2+1} =
					\left.-\arctan \frac{1}{\sigma}\right|_{\sigma =s}^{\sigma =\infty} = \arctan \frac{1}{s}
				</md>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</example>
	<p>
		Some types of differential equations involve the terms <m>ty\,'(t)</m> or <m>ty\,''(t)</m>. Laplace transforms can be used
		to find the solution if we use the additional substitutions
		<md>
			<mrow xml:id="Laplacety1" number="yes">\mathcal{L}\big(ty\,'(t)\big) \amp = -sY\,'(s) - Y(s), \quad \text{and}</mrow>
			<mrow xml:id="Laplacety2" number="yes">\mathcal{L}\big(ty\,''(t)\big) \amp = -s^2Y\,'(s) - 2sY(s) + y(0)</mrow>
		</md>.
	</p>
	<example>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use Laplace transforms to solve the initial value problem
				<md>
					ty\,''(t) - ty\,'(t) - y(t) = 0, \quad \text{with} \quad y(0) = 0
				</md>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>Y(s)</m> denote the Laplace transform of <m>y(t)</m>, then using the 
				<xref ref="Laplacety1">substitutions</xref> and <xref ref="Laplacety2"></xref> results in 
				<md number="yes" xml:id="LaplaceTransDE">
					\hspace{-0.3in}-s^2Y\,'(s) -2sY(s) +0+sY\,'(s) + Y(s) - Y(s) = 0
				</md>.
			</p>
			<p>
				This equation involves <m>Y\,'(s)</m> and can be written as a first-order linear differential equation:
				<md number="yes" xml:id="TransDE">
					Y\,'(s) + \left(\frac{2}{s-1}\right)Y(s) = 0
				</md>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The integrating factor <m>\rho</m> for the differential equation is
				<md>
					\rho = \exp\left(\int \frac{2}{s-1}\,ds\right) = e^{2\ln (s-1)}=(s-1)^2
				</md>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Multiplying Equation <xref ref="TransDE"/> by <m>\rho</m> produces
				<md>
					(s-1)^2Y\,'(s) +2(s-1) Y(s) = \frac{d}{ds}\big[(s-1)^2Y(s)\big] = 0
				</md>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Now we integrate the equation <m>\frac{d}{ds}\big[(s-1)^2Y(s)\big] = 0</m> with respect to <m>s</m> and the results is
				<m>(s-1)^2Y(s) =C</m>, where <m>C</m> is the constant of integration. Hence the solution to 
				<xref ref="LaplaceTransDE">Equation</xref> is
				<md>
					Y(s) = \frac{C}{(s-1)^2}
				</md>.
				The inverse of the transform <m>Y(s)</m> in equation is the desired solution
				<md>
					y(t) = Cte^t
				</md>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</example>
<exercises xml:id="exercises_mult-and-division-by-t">
<title>Exercises</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the Laplace transform for each of the following:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(te^{-2t})</m>;
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(te^{-2t}) = \frac{1}{(s+2)^2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t^2e^{4t})</m>;
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t\sin 3t)</m>;
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t\sin 3t) = \frac{6s}{(s^2+9)^2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t^2\cos 2t)</m>;
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t\sinh t)</m>;
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t\sinh t) = \frac{2s}{(s^2-1)^2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\mathcal{L}(t^2\cosh t)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Show that <m>\mathcal{L}(\frac{1-\cos t}{t}) = \ln \frac{s^2}{s^2+1}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Show that <m>\mathcal{L}(\frac{e^t-1}{t}) = \ln \frac{s}{s-1}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find <m>\mathcal{L}(te^{at}\cos bt)</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find <m>\mathcal{L}(t\sin bt)</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<m>\mathcal{L}(t\sin bt) = \frac{2bs}{(s^2+b^2)^2}</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
      <statement>
			<p>
				Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}(\ln \frac{s}{s+1})</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\big(\ln \frac{s^2+1}{(s-1)^2}\big)</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\Big(\ln \frac{s^2+1}{(s-1)^2}\Big) = \frac{2(e^t-\cos t)}{t}</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Solve the initial value problem for each of the following:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>y\,''(t) +2y\,'(t) + y(t) = 2e^{-t},</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0 \quad \text{and} \quad y\,'(0)=1;</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>y(t) = te^{-t}+t^2e^{-t}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>y\,''(t) + y(t) = 2\sin t,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0 \quad \text{and} \quad y\,'(0)=-1;</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>ty\,''(t) - ty\,'(t) - y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0;</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>y(t) = Cte^t</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>ty\,''(t) + (t-1)y\,'(t) - 2y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0;</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>ty\,''(t) + ty\,'(t) - y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0;</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<m>y(t) = Ct</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>ty\,''(t) + (t-1)y\,'(t) + y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=0.</m>
					</p>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Solve the Laguerre equation <m>ty\,''(t) + (1-t)y\,'(t) + y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=1.</m>
					</p>
			</p>
		</statement>
      <solution>
			<p>
				<m>y(t) = 1-t</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Solve the Laguerre equation <m>ty\,''(t) + (1-t)y\,'(t) + 2y(t) = 0,</m>
					<p>
						<m>\qquad \text{with} \quad y(0)=1.</m>
					</p>
			</p>
		</statement>
	</exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
